    "result_text": "Sheets now have roles and a clerk limit, and a clerk who tries to settle a claim with a due above the limit is refused. All 4 acceptance tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it behaves**\n- **Roles:** any user name is a clerk unless set to steward on that sheet. Each role change writes `ROLE_SET sheet=\u2026 by=\u2026 user=\u2026 role=\u2026` to the audit file. A role other than clerk or steward (e.g. \"boss\") shows `role-form-error` and nothing is saved.\n- **Clerk limit:** stored with two decimals, and blank means no limit. Saving writes `SETTING_CHANGED sheet=\u2026 by=\u2026 key=clerk-limit value=\u2026`. Saving the same value again still writes a record, which one of the tests expects.\n- **Settling:** the claim's due is checked against the limit before anything is saved. Above the limit, a clerk gets `claim-error` with \"That claim's due is above the clerk limit, so only a steward can settle it.\" Dues of n/a always pass, stewards can settle anything, and the rule that whoever opened a claim can't settle it still applies.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **Data:** migration `V19__roles_and_clerk_limit.sql` adds a nullable `clerk_limit` column to `sheet` and a new `sheet_role` table.\n- **Server:** a new `setting` package (`Role`, `SheetRole`, a repository, `SettingService`, `SettingController`) with get and save endpoints for roles and settings. `ClaimService.changeStatus` now calls `requireMaySettle` when settling.\n- **Front end:** a new Settings section (`features/settings/`), shown on the home screen above People. It reloads whenever the sheet is loaded.\n\n**Things to know**\n- A refused settle comes back from the server as HTTP 422, which the claim row turns into the message above. 422 was already used for \"dated after the person retired\", but only when adding or editing a claim, so the two don't collide.\n- My first edit to the claim error messages also put the clerk-limit message into the add/edit form's error handling by mistake. I removed it and re-ran the tests, which still pass.",
